    "case_summary": "A young black woman presented with painful lesions on both legs. She reported chills, bilateral lower extremity swelling, and several small, painful, 'pimple-like bumps' appearing on her bilateral lower legs, which ulcerated several days later. The ulcers progressed despite a recent course of trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for cultures growing methicillin-sensitive Staphylococcus aureus. Medical history includes treatment with methimazole and atenolol for the past year. No other medications or supplements were reported.\n\nBilateral lower extremities revealed pitting edema and multiple discrete, round, dry ulcers, most with central eschars, dusky gray borders, and collarettes of scale. During follow-up, an intact 4-mm papular lesion was identified on her lower right leg. No pathergy at the previous punch biopsy site was observed.\n\n- Laboratory tests:     - Elevated C-reactive protein     - Increased erythrocyte sedimentation rate    - Normal C3 and C4 complement levels    - ANA titer 1:80 (reference range, <1:40)    - P-ANCA titer 1:20 (reference range, <1:20)    - Anti-myeloperoxidase (MPO) titer 141 U (reference range, <2.8 U)    - Anti-SSA/Ro antibodies level 120 U (reference range, 0-40 U)  - Pathology:    - Initial punch biopsy: Mid- and deep dermal marked lymphohistiocytic infiltrate with neutrophils and focal abscess    - Second biopsy of intact papule: Intraepidermal and dermal perivascular neutrophilic infiltrate with marked papillary dermal edema, necrosis of vessel walls, and focal fibrinoid necrosis    - Special stains (PAS-diastase, GMS, Brown-Brenn, AFB): Negative for organisms  - Images:    Image A: Discrete, round, dry ulcers with central eschar and raised borders on left lateral lower leg    Image B: A new, 4-mm intact papule on right medial lower leg    Images C and D: Histopathological images from punch biopsy specimen of the papule, hematoxylin-eosin",
